Fixed the evaluator's finding: there is now a real, committed src/ change.

**What was wrong:** the previous attempt left the tree clean — no src change existed; the only recent commit was an unrelated social-state update.

What I implemented (src/safety.rs, commit 2d496eb7, 71 insertions):

A closed hole in the /read and /plan mode write guard. detect_write_command special-cased `sed -i but not perl -i, so perl -pi -e 's/a/b/' file` — a routine in-place file mutation — passed the guard silently (the command token is perl, which is not in WRITE_VERBS).

• `perl_edits_in_place(segment) — scans only perl's leading switch run and stops at the first non-flag token, so perl script.pl -i (where -i` is the script's flag) is not flagged. Cluster scanning stops at argument-taking switches (PERL_ARG_TAKING_SWITCHES), so the lowercase i inside -I/opt/lib or -MList::Util isn't mistaken for the in-place switch. Uses strip_prefix, no byte indexing.
• Wired into detect_write_command with an honest message naming what matched ( perl -i edits files in place ), consistent with the existing sed -i refusal.
• Tests added to the existing fixture tables: 3 positives (-pi, -i.bak, sudo perl -i) and 6 negatives (-ne, -e, -M…, -I…, perl script.pl -i, grep perl file), plus a message-content assertion.

Verification (all run, all green): `cargo fmt -- --check OK, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings clean, cargo build OK, cargo test` 4618 + 89 passed / 0 failed. Working tree clean after commit.
